Watch the protocol run. This page climbs three levels: Zig chapters that take
one layer at a time, C programs over the same ABI, and cross-language apps
holding one real [OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) conversation. Every
example is a build target, so the code here cannot drift from the library.

The hard moves each get a chapter. Chapter 02 is three-party handoff, where a
call settles as the introduced capability, and chapter 06 is promise pipelining
plus attenuation. If the ideas are new, read [Concepts]($link.page('concepts'))
first.

## The Zig chapters

They read top-down. Chapter 01 is the surface an application rides, and
chapter 02 widens it to the multi-session CapTP `Host` tier. Chapters 03
through 05 then descend one layer at a time to the raw engine, and chapter 06
returns to the actor layer for pipelining and attenuation.

| # | Chapter | What it shows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 01 | [connect & serve](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig) | the application surface over a real TCP socket: listen, accept, publish by name, serve; connect, fetch, call, switch on the outcome |
| 02 | [introduce and redeem](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig) | the capability pattern itself: an introduction settles as the introduced capability (the host redeems the gift inside the call), and the direct call leaves the introducer behind |
| 03 | [actor far-send](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/03_actor_far_send.zig) | the `Vat` pattern beneath a call: publish an actor, far-send to it, settle the promise |
| 04 | [look up by name](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/04_look_up_by_name.zig) | what `fetch("name")` does underneath: asking the session's front-door object (the *bootstrap object*) for a name by its unguessable token (its *swiss number*) |
| 05 | [call a remote method](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/05_call_a_remote_method.zig) | the raw engine: pair two peers and shuttle deliver and resolve one `step` at a time |
| 06 | [pipeline and attenuate](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/zig/06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig) | promise pipelining and attenuation together: dependent calls aimed at an unresolved answer, and a reply that forwards only the allow-listed verbs |

`zig build examples` builds and runs them all, and they also run under
`zig build check`. The
[example chapters](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/README.md)
carry the full source and a few lower-level mechanics besides.

## The C programs

The C programs prove the same library from the other side of the C ABI, and
they build and run under the same `zig build examples` step as the chapters.

| Program | What it shows |
| --- | --- |
| [echo_peer.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/echo_peer.c) | a full in-process echo conversation between two peers, the reference shape of a C host event loop over the sans-io surface |
| [frame_view.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/frame_view.c) | frame ownership over the C ABI: a borrowed frame view over caller-owned bytes |
| [context_alloc.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/context_alloc.c) | a C embedder owning every pancake allocation through the context's host-allocator callbacks |
| [compose_transports.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/compose_transports.c) | the à-la-carte archives composing: three headers, one link line, no symbol collisions |
| [host_embedder_pump.c](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/c/host_embedder_pump.c) | the byte-fed multi-session CapTP host over the C ABI: feed inbound frames, take outbound, and answer the host's own actions, so a full CapTP product runs from C with no socket |

## The cross-language apps

Each app is built on the same small surface of application verbs (connect,
publish, fetch, send, serve) written once per language, so the app reads the
same in Zig and the four [bindings]($link.page('bindings')): Rust, Python,
Racket, and TypeScript.

| App | What it is | Run it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [rooms](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/apps/rooms/) | a capability-secure chat, real-time, with a browser UI and direct messages by introduction | `zig build demo-rooms` |
| rooms over iroh | the same chat over encrypted QUIC | `zig build demo-rooms-iroh` (needs cargo) |
| [tasks](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/apps/tasks/) | a shared task board: CRUD plus live state | `zig build demo-tasks` |
| [tools](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/apps/tools/) | a capability showcase for AI agent harnesses: delegation, attenuation, revocation | `zig build demo-tools` |
| [accountability](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/apps/accountability-on-pancake/) | a signed-call accountability app over a real OCapN session on the `tcp` netlayer | `zig build demo-accountability` |

`rooms` is the fullest live picture. `zig build rooms-web` serves the browser
UI at `http://localhost:8000`. Pick a member and choose _Message_: the room
runs a three-party handoff, then steps out of the path, and the conversation
header says so. `zig build rooms-web-iroh` moves the peer-to-peer legs onto
[iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')), while the browser's own leg still rides
its WebSocket. App code does not change between the two; only the address does.

The `rooms`, `tasks`, and `tools` demos need `deno` beside Zig, and the other
languages join when their runtimes are present. `accountability` runs a Zig
host with Deno and Python callers, and it does not take `-Dattach`. `-Dport`
pins a port. The demos run as siblings of `zig build check`, not inside it.
